 2. 📺 Scoop: Government-funded ads &quot;just beginning&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A frame from one of the government-paid ads. Image : The White House/YouTube President Trump is personally selecting which government-funded ads his administration is airing ahead of the midterms — with more to come, Axios' Alex Isenstadt has learned. 

 "This is just the beginning," an administration official told Axios about the Trump-glorifying ads, which pop up during college football, NFL games and other popular programs. Why it matters:  The adulatory thrust of the taxpayer-funded ads has drawn bipartisan pushback, with legal experts calling them  propaganda .

 Behind the scenes: Trump — a former reality TV producer who has long fixated on how things play on television — has taken a hands-on role in the ad campaign.

 The ads are being produced inside the White House. Trump liked videos the White House digital team — which regularly churns out aggressive , war-room-style social media posts — was producing and began posting them to his Truth Social account. He then decided he wanted to put them on TV and picked the videos he wanted to air. The decision was made a couple of weeks ago, and the ads began airing Sept. 23. The Department of Homeland Security is placing the ad buys using money from an existing budget, an administration official told us. 

 DHS has set aside $20 million for the ads, per AP . The Wall Street Journal reports the money is coming from U.S. Customs and Border Protection. The White House says : "Patriotism isn't partisan. These public service announcements are unapologetically American, clearly disclosed, and aimed at the country — not a line on the ballot." 💸 State of play: The government has spent at least $2.5 million airing the ads, the ad-tracking firm AdImpact estimates.

 The ads have aired more than 2,000 times on broadcast networks and more than 50 times on cable. The single biggest program outlay was on Fox's NFL coverage over the weekend, with 340 airings. Share this story . 

 
 
