 4. 🧍‍♂️ AI agents&#x27; normal-people problem 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Stock: Getty Images AI companies are staking their future on the mass adoption of agents, hoping that they can solve the annoyances of modern life — email, travel bookings, online purchases, Axios' Shane Savitsky writes. 

 Why it matters: The frenzy around Meta's Muse shows there's serious consumer curiosity about the technology. But there's little evidence that most Americans are willing to invest the time needed to re-engineer their lives in a way that puts AI at the center. Driving the news: Meta's Muse vaulted to the top of Apple's App Store on the back of its integrations with Facebook and Instagram and a cute, fuzzy mascot.

 Microsoft released a souped-up Copilot with an agentic Autopilot feature, while OpenAI yesterday announced its own agentic assistant, dots. Reality check: Agents only become truly useful when people hand them access to their digital lives — and people really don't want to.

 A 13-country   Thales poll  this year found broad pushback against the core uses of agents: Only 13% of respondents would let an "AI helper" read their emails, 11% would let one rebook travel, and just 7% would let it move money between bank accounts. Share this story . 

 
 
