 6. 💡 CEO group defends infrastructure from AI attacks 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 JPMorgan Chase CEO Jamie Dimon and Citi CEO Jane Fraser testify before Congress in 2023. Photo: Saul Loeb/AFP via Getty Images A recently formed CEO-led coalition on critical infrastructure has added top leaders from key pillars of the American economy. 

 Why it matters: Growing concern about the prospect of AI-powered attacks on public infrastructure, financial institutions and essential businesses is fueling momentum for action, Axios' Nathan Bomey writes. 🔋 The Alliance for Critical Infrastructure, chaired by JPMorgan Chase CEO Jamie Dimon, added members with heft and depth from multiple industries to ensure that AI doesn't wreak havoc: 

 Financial: Citi CEO Jane Fraser, Bank of America CEO Brian Moynihan and Goldman Sachs CEO David Solomon. Tech: Amazon CEO Andy Jassy, Apple CEO John Ternus, Google CEO Sundar Pichai and Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella. Energy and water: ExxonMobil CEO Darren Woods, Chevron CEO Mike Wirth, Southern Co. CEO Chris Womack and Xcel Energy CEO Bob Frenzel. Communications: AT&amp;T CEO John Stankey, Verizon CEO Dan Schulman, T-Mobile CEO Srini Gopalan, Comcast CEO Michael Angelakis and Charter Communications CEO Chris Winfrey. Transportation: Delta Air Lines CEO Ed Bastian, United Airlines CEO Scott Kirby, American Airlines CEO Robert Isom, UPS CEO Carol Tomé and FedEx CEO Raj Subramaniam. Share this story . 

 
 
